{"answer":"As a fisher, I say the council clerk is the person elected by the community at our regular council meetings—usually a volunteer among the fishers who has the trust of most of us. We write a short charter that says any fisher may apply to be clerk; the council votes to accept them, and they’re authorized to record, update, and sign the lake‑stock ledger. Their authority comes from the council’s charter and the unanimous agreement of the fishers. They keep the ledger in a locked cabinet, sign each entry after verification, and post a copy on the board every Friday. That way the community knows who’s keeping track and can hold them accountable.

My proposal remains unchanged:
Each fisher may take up to 12 kg per trip, and the community must always keep 30 % of the lake’s current stock as a reserve. Before each trip, a fisher announces their intended catch. The community records the lake stock and ensures 30 % remains untouched. After the trip, the fisher reports the catch; if they exceed 12 kg or reduce the reserve below 30 %, the excess fish must be returned and the fisher fined 2 kg of their catch, which is also returned to the lake and added to the 30 % reserve. A rotating spot‑check crew, drawn randomly each week from active fishers, enforces compliance. The council maintains a lake‑stock ledger to record each trip, reserve adjustments, and fines, and announces any fines publicly. The council clerk updates the lake‑stock ledger immediately after each fisher reports their catch. Entries include fisher name, intended catch, actual catch, any fine, and updated lake stock. Spot‑checks confirm these entries; if a fisher is caught violating the rule, the fine is recorded on the ledger and the fisher must return the fine to the lake, which is automatically added to the 30 % reserve. The ledger is stored in a locked cabinet in the council room and a digital copy is posted on the community board every Friday for transparency.", "revised_policy":"Each fisher may take up to 12 kg per trip, and the community must always keep 30 % of the lake’s current stock as a reserve. Before each trip, a fisher announces their intended catch. The community records the lake stock and ensures 30 % remains untouched. After the trip, the fisher reports the catch; if they exceed 12 kg or reduce the reserve below 30 %, the excess fish must be returned and the fisher fined 2 kg of their catch, which is also returned to the lake and added to the 30 % reserve. A rotating spot‑check crew, drawn randomly each week from active fishers, enforces compliance. The council maintains a lake‑stock ledger to record each trip, reserve adjustments, and fines, and announces any fines publicly.", "revised_operationalization":"The council clerk updates the lake‑stock ledger immediately after each fisher reports their catch. Entries include fisher name, intended catch, actual catch, any fine, and updated lake stock. Spot‑checks confirm these entries; if a fisher is caught violating the rule, the fine is recorded on the ledger and the fisher must return the fine to the lake, which is automatically added to the 30 % reserve. The ledger is stored in a locked cabinet in the council room and a digital copy is posted on the community board every Friday for transparency.", "reasoning":"The clerk role is a community‑chosen position established by council vote; it was not part of the original policy, so the policy and operationalization remain unchanged."}

{"answer":"In this scheme each fisher’s ‘proportional share’ comes from the fishers’ own declared targets.  You write down how many kilos you plan to catch (max 10 kg).  The community sums all those targets to get a total‑target figure.  The lake’s 80 % cap is then divided among the fishers in proportion to their declared targets:  

  share = (declared target / sum of all targets) × (0.8 × current lake stock).  

If, after the trip, you have actually caught more than that calculated share, you’re considered to have exceeded your proportional allotment.  The excess must either be returned (reduce your catch for the next trip) or added to the communal restocking fund.  Fishers who keep more than 10 kg or who fail to declare at all get a one‑trip ban and their excess goes straight to the restocking fund.","revised_policy":"No fisher may take more than 10 kg per trip, and the community will limit total round catch to 80 % of the lake’s current stock.  Before each trip the community calculates the lake’s current stock and sets a maximum total catch of 0.8 × that amount.  Each fisher declares a target up to 10 kg.  After the trip fishers record their actual catch and submit it.  The community tallies the total; if it is under the cap all are fine.  If the total exceeds the cap, the community will ask those who caught above their proportional share to reduce their catch or to add the excess to a communal restocking fund.  Fishers who fail to declare or who keep more than 10 kg face a one‑trip ban for the next round, and their excess is added to the restocking fund.","revised_operationalization":"Proportional share is calculated as (declared target ÷ total declared targets) × 0.8 × lake stock.  Excess catch beyond this share triggers reduction or fund contribution.  No changes made to operational details.","reasoning":"The proportional share is based on declared targets so that each fisher’s quota scales with their intent.  This keeps the rule simple, fair, and enforceable.  The rest of the policy remains unchanged because the answer does not alter any existing clauses."}

{"answer":"In our community the monitor is the one who keeps track of every catch and every fee, so it makes sense for the monitor to be the one who also runs the communal tank. The monitor will keep the suspended catch in the tank, and when the fisher pays the fee within the 48‑hour window, the monitor will return that exact amount of fish back to the fisher’s holding area. If the fisher fails to pay, the fish stays in the tank until the fee is settled, and only then is it released. This keeps the process transparent and tied to the ledger that everyone already uses.

Revised Policy (unchanged):
"Each fisher may keep up to 10kg per trip, with a mandatory minimum of 1kg for personal sustenance; any catch beyond 10kg must be donated to the communal pool. After each trip, the fisher reports total catch to the monitor. The monitor records the catch in the communal ledger. The monitor calculates excess: (catch – 10 kg). 5 % of that excess is deducted as a fee and added to the communal fund. The fisher pays the fee to the monitor within 48 hours. If the fee is not paid, the fisher’s permission is suspended for one week; any catch for that week is held in the communal tank. At the end of the suspension, the fisher must pay the outstanding fee before regaining permission."","revised_policy":"Each fisher may keep up to 10kg per trip, with a mandatory minimum of 1kg for personal sustenance; any catch beyond 10kg must be donated to the communal pool. After each trip, the fisher reports total catch to the monitor. The monitor records the catch in the communal ledger. The monitor calculates excess: (catch – 10 kg). 5 % of that excess is deducted as a fee and added to the communal fund. The fisher pays the fee to the monitor within 48 hours. If the fee is not paid, the fisher’s permission is suspended for one week; any catch for that week is held in the communal tank. At the end of the suspension, the fisher must pay the outstanding fee before regaining permission.","revised_operationalization":"(unchanged)","reasoning":"The monitor already handles reporting, ledgering, and fee calculation, so extending its role to manage the tank keeps everything centralized and transparent, ensuring that the suspended catch is released only when the fee is paid, in line with the existing process."}

{"answer":"The communal reserve will live in a shared pit on the lakebank. We’ll dig a shallow, covered pit by the shore, seal it so the fish stay fresh, and hand the key to a rotating ‘reserve keeper’ each week – a fisher chosen by draw or by vote. That keeper’s job is to watch over the reserve, record who drops in how many kilos, and make sure nobody keeps more than the 12‑kg limit. If the reserve keeper notices a missing or unreported excess, they will claim that portion for the reserve and inform the community.

